WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.440
Every week, dozens of new manga chapters arrive&nbsp;
to entertain all of us consumers

00:00:05.609 --> 00:00:09.617
Ju Jutsu Kaisen, My Hero, One Piece, Hajime no Ippo 

00:00:09.917 --> 00:00:15.095
I can go on&nbsp;and on about the manga that I fervently wait&nbsp;for, be it weekly or monthly 

00:00:15.945 --> 00:00:21.443
But something that&nbsp;often goes forgotten is what goes on behind&nbsp;that awaited weekly release

00:00:21.901 --> 00:00:26.401
 The blood, sweat,&nbsp;and tears that manga authors–or mangakas–&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.401 --> 00:00:34.538
put into creating a mere 20 pages. But what is just&nbsp;20 pages, a couple minutes out of our day for&nbsp;us readers,

00:00:34.857 --> 00:00:40.640
is a week of non-stop work, little&nbsp;
sleep, malnutrition, and stress for the author&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:46.240
anime like Bakuman have shed light on&nbsp;
the life of a mangaka in a very realistic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:53.040
way and the sad truth is that being a manga&nbsp;
writer is essentially voluntary enslavement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.760 --> 00:01:02.746
Meeting deadlines, living up to fan expectations,&nbsp;company expectations, and their own expectations&nbsp;as writers thinking up a plot 

00:01:03.668 --> 00:01:06.960
Today I will&nbsp;go into the sad and dark reality of being a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:11.840
manga author and the extreme toll it's&nbsp;
taken on countless authors to this day

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:22.160
After getting picked up by a big publishing&nbsp;
company like Shonen Jump, though you would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:29.040
expect it to get easier from there, this is far&nbsp;
from the truth for mangakas. There are no breaks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:35.646
The weekly schedule for most mangakas relies on&nbsp;
less than six hours of sleep every day 

00:01:36.302 --> 00:01:40.922
A number&nbsp;that reduces usually the bigger and more popular&nbsp;
the manga is

00:01:40.922 --> 00:01:42.629
Eichiro Oda (yes ik it's the wrong picture lol) 

00:01:42.629 --> 00:01:46.802
the author of One Piece and the biggest manga in the history of the world,&nbsp;

00:01:47.065 --> 00:01:51.861
is reported to on average sleep only 3 hours from&nbsp;2am to 5am 

00:01:52.336 --> 00:01:58.945
Another source claims that these three&nbsp;
hours actually consist of only 30-minute naps&nbsp;every four hours 

00:01:59.583 --> 00:02:02.690
So, why is time so limited? 

00:02:03.534 --> 00:02:06.480
Well, you have to imagine creating a short story from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:10.928
start to finish all within one week and all of the&nbsp;
processes involved 

00:02:11.322 --> 00:02:16.784
After turning in the previous&nbsp;week's chapter on sunday, the cycle begins anew.&nbsp;Immediately.

00:02:17.121 --> 00:02:25.920
According to an anonymous mangaka's&nbsp;schedule, from noon on Sunday to 5am on Tuesday&nbsp;it consists almost entirely of storyboarding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:32.521
Deciding on panels, what characters appear in each,&nbsp;the story of the chapter, how it ends&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.952 --> 00:02:40.400
Essentially planing out its entire structure. The only break&nbsp;in that long period of time is two hours for sleep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:46.400
and one hour for breakfast – that is as long as&nbsp;
you consider meetings with their editors as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:51.360
still work of course. And you can tell from&nbsp;
the rest of the week just how packed it is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:58.880
coloring the panels, shading, working on the final&nbsp;manuscript, only three hours of free time exist in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:04.062
the entire week excluding eating and sleeping&nbsp;
which are the bare necessities 

00:03:04.935 --> 00:03:11.132
But again, to&nbsp;return to the idea of voluntary enslavement that&nbsp;I mentioned earlier–because it does seem rather&nbsp;harsh–

00:03:11.713 --> 00:03:19.383
we have to remember that it is voluntary.&nbsp;
Mangakas, despite the terrible schedule, do this&nbsp;all for a reason

00:03:19.834 --> 00:03:24.240
Yoshiro Togashi, the author&nbsp;
of Hunter x Hunter, which is notorious for taking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:30.720
long periods of hiatus, stated that, "It has come to&nbsp;
the point where either the story concludes first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:37.840
or I die before that happens. But... I do intend&nbsp;
to finish it" that end game looks rather bleak&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:42.960
considering Hunter x Hunter's current hiatus chart&nbsp;
lines with primarily breaks for the past years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:47.133
but we can never underestimate the passion&nbsp;of a mangaka

00:03:47.846 --> 00:03:52.962
Likewise, Oda told his current&nbsp;editor when he first joined to, "Die for One Piece"&nbsp;

00:03:53.775 --> 00:03:56.880
Mangakas are extremely dedicated to their craft&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.640 --> 00:04:02.880
Of course sleep deprivation isn't the&nbsp;
only cost of being a mangaka either&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:10.160
Sitting in a chair for so long working on manga&nbsp;leads to numerous health hazards, they can develop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:15.695
diabetes, despite almost all mangakas being very&nbsp;skinny and almost frail 

00:04:16.301 --> 00:04:19.272
Tendinitis can develop in&nbsp;their hands from drawing so long 

00:04:19.553 --> 00:04:22.480
Something&nbsp;that is so common amongst mangakas that it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.480 --> 00:04:27.707
isn't even an expectation that they'll get it&nbsp;
they just accept it as a way of life 

00:04:28.468 --> 00:04:34.560
One of the members of Clamp, a female mangaka collective&nbsp;
who created Card Captor Sakura among other names,

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:40.800
suffered from lumbar compression fracture in&nbsp;
2011 because she spent too many hours hunched&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:43.513
over her chair and straining her back drawing&nbsp;

00:04:44.245 --> 00:04:47.040
Tagashi, the author of Hunter x Hunter also deals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:52.640
with aggravated back pain that prevents him&nbsp;
from consistently putting out chapters 

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:57.920
Other&nbsp;series like Vagabond and Berserk, despite being&nbsp;
some of the most highly rated manga of all time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.480 --> 00:05:02.503
also suffered an early fate due to health concerns&nbsp;
of the authors 

00:05:03.479 --> 00:05:08.732
This culture of overworking&nbsp;yourself isn't exclusive to mangakas however,&nbsp;

00:05:08.939 --> 00:05:12.973
 
as the concept of Karoshi, "Death by Overwork"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.360 --> 00:05:21.585
is very prevalent across all of japan. In 2016,
it was estimated that one in five workers&nbsp;were at risk of Karoshi

00:05:22.110 --> 00:05:25.840
This is so serious of an&nbsp;
issue in the country in fact, that the government&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:32.080
passed a reform bill in 2018 that limited&nbsp;
overtime and fined violators for overworking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:39.680
Japan is a very collectivism-oriented country,&nbsp;
prioritizing their group's success over individual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.480 --> 00:05:47.040
Kona Shiomachi made an illustration in response&nbsp;
to the 2015 death of an employee due to Karoshi&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.600 --> 00:05:54.721
the key line being, "If I move one step further&nbsp;
in front of the train, I wouldn't have to go to&nbsp;work tomorrow"&nbsp;

00:05:55.884 --> 00:06:00.320
The idea of Karoshi is reflected&nbsp;
in people dedicating themselves to their work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.320 --> 00:06:06.720
and employers. To an extent that is often not&nbsp;
just prioritizing group success over individual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.280 --> 00:06:14.240
but it comes at the cost of individual health&nbsp;
as well, be it physical or just mental well-being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.960 --> 00:06:20.960
Kishimoto Masashi the creator of Naruto, while&nbsp;
the series was still being serialized never had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.960 --> 00:06:25.763
the time to take his wife on a honeymoon despite&nbsp;
being married for 15 years 

00:06:26.364 --> 00:06:33.440
Tite Kubo, the author of&nbsp;Bleach, once spoke in an interview at San Diego&nbsp;Comic-Con on how his appearance at Comic-Con&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:39.280
was far from a vacation, as he had to draw any&nbsp;chapters he missed before he went on the trip&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:44.800
Which considering the anonymous typical schedule&nbsp;
for our mangaka that we looked at earlier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.800 --> 00:06:51.120
I have no idea how he was able to fit it all in,&nbsp;
just another testament to a mangaka's dedication&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:57.803
And of course these problems exist&nbsp;
on the condition that the mangaka&nbsp;does make it

00:06:58.122 --> 00:07:02.929
Their manga makes it into Shonen&nbsp;
Jump, or another renowned publisher, and they get&nbsp;popular

00:07:03.501 --> 00:07:07.680
Because if they don't get popular&nbsp;
enough then they can't afford assistants, a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:13.600
valuable asset for any manga writer and have to&nbsp;
do all of the work themselves, filling in lines&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.600 --> 00:07:20.800
backgrounds, shading, all of it on their own. The&nbsp;
pay is also nearly criminal, the assistant to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.800 --> 00:07:26.240
writer of Prince of Tennis gave the figure at&nbsp;
about $100 per page, and though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.240 --> 00:07:30.960
that could mean about $8000 per&nbsp;
month for a weekly author producing 20 pages a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:37.760
week, this is before taxes and hiring assistants&nbsp;
which will of course detract heavily from it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.320 --> 00:07:43.760
Though it isn't necessarily terrible, relative&nbsp;
to the insane schedule they have and overwork&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.760 --> 00:07:50.080
it is certainly not comparatively enough, getting&nbsp;
an anime adaptation can also drastically improve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.080 --> 00:07:57.798
the exposure and popularity of the manga, but then&nbsp;
as we see with one piece for example it is still&nbsp;far from easy

00:07:58.534 --> 00:08:04.080
Oda (wrong pic again my b) is one of the most hard-working&nbsp;
and overworked by extent mangakas out there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.800 --> 00:08:10.000
No matter how big your manga, is it&nbsp;
is always rigorous and constant work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.680 --> 00:08:18.400
And this all continues until your series finishes&nbsp;
whether it is of your own accord or not because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.400 --> 00:08:24.240
it isn't just as simple as writing a chapter&nbsp;
of one big book, but instead creating a new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.240 --> 00:08:30.400
story every week that has to attract consistent&nbsp;
attention and remain exciting and interesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.280 --> 00:08:35.040
Otherwise if the fans don't like&nbsp;
it, the company is quick to drop you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.680 --> 00:08:41.187
after all there's no shortage of new manga&nbsp;
coming out in Japan they can always get&nbsp;someone else

00:08:41.901 --> 00:08:45.920
On the other hand, in anime like&nbsp;
Bakuman we saw how the company pressured the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.920 --> 00:08:51.680
mangaka to continue the series despite the author&nbsp;
feeling like it reached its natural end already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.240 --> 00:08:58.000
Sometimes companies will do this because the manga&nbsp;
is so popular that they can't afford to lose it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.000 --> 00:09:04.725
which means that sometimes even the author of&nbsp;
the series themselves don't have full control&nbsp;over their own story

00:09:05.569 --> 00:09:10.240
However, this isn't a super&nbsp;
common occurrence we see authors like Tite Kubo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.240 --> 00:09:15.200
who decided himself that he would draw Bleach&nbsp;
to the conclusion he wanted despite health&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.200 --> 00:09:21.821
concerns and critiques from the editors, which&nbsp;
is again another reminder that this "enslavement"&nbsp;

00:09:21.821 --> 00:09:24.269
is still voluntary at the end of the day 

00:09:25.601 --> 00:09:28.880
When&nbsp;you look at everything put together, it's a rather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.880 --> 00:09:36.480
unavoidable problem. Be it due to collectivist&nbsp;
cultural standards, Karoshi, meeting deadlines&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.480 --> 00:09:42.720
or just the mangaka's own desire to finish what&nbsp;
they started, the process of creating manga is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.280 --> 00:09:49.280
difficult to say the least. We can only hope as&nbsp;
readers that the reward they get from working&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.280 --> 00:09:54.401
creating something that resonates with us fans&nbsp;
and succeeds is enough for them 

00:09:54.767 --> 00:10:02.000
Manga is truly the&nbsp;life work of anyone creating it, and so we do have&nbsp;
to be grateful for everything they've done for&nbsp;us

00:10:03.840 --> 00:10:06.640
